All six CIVO-positive seeds cross the $\nve > 1.5\times$ threshold (Table~\ref{tab:post_civo}), yet the downstream damage is stochastic.\footnote{Exp\_009e was excluded from multi-seed analyses due to a seed configuration error (default seed=0, identical to exp\_008d).}  Full collapse is the majority outcome (5/6 seeds), with one self-correction case (Walker seed~2) that remains severely degraded (29.3\% of baseline). Cheetah seeds exhibit more extreme overestimation (peak NVE $8.27$--$108.52\times$ vs.\ Walker's $1.53$--$4.62\times$), consistent with the Cheetah reward scale amplifying the fidelity gap.  We hypothesize that the divergence relates to the critic's representational capacity at the time of CIVO onset.  \citet{lyle2022capacity} demonstrate that deep RL networks suffer progressive capacity loss, in which early-learned features become increasingly difficult to overwrite.  If the critic's capacity is already partially consumed when CIVO injects overestimation, the network cannot recalibrate (seed~0); if sufficient capacity remains, the critic can absorb transient overestimation and recover (seed~2).

\subsubsection{Failure and Degradation Mode Taxonomy}

Table~\ref{tab:failure_modes} classifies the seven distinct failure and degradation modes observed across all experimental configurations. The taxonomy reveals that CIVO is not a single failure mode but a family of related pathologies, with the trained $\dt$-embedding creating vulnerability through at least three independent mechanisms: value-driven collapse (monotonic, late-onset, and SGS-resistant CIVO), entropy-driven instability (IRD collapse), and gradient-mediated effects (gradient starvation, SGS-paradoxical worsening).

